Found by the SW-328 private port: a `let` value used more than once in exported code had its defining call inlined at every use, so the work ran repeatedly (whole merge embedded 5 times in one 46 KB line; 326 ms vs 25 ms at 100 items). Workaround: pass the value through a helper's parameter.

Repro (compare the generated JS of viaLet vs viaHelper):
```
import Std (List, Option)

def work (n: Nat): List Nat := (List.range n).map (i => i + 1)

def decode (xs: List Nat): List Nat := xs.map (i => i + 0)

def encode (xs: List Nat): Option (List Nat) :=
  let e := xs.map (i => i + 1)
  if decode e == xs then Some e else None

export def viaHelper (n: Nat): Option (List Nat) := encode (work n)

export def viaLet (n: Nat): Option (List Nat) :=
  let m := work n
  let e := m.map (i => i + 1)
  if decode e == m then Some e else None
```

Fix: the JS inliner must not duplicate a non-trivial let-bound value; add an extract regression test that counts calls.

Found by the SW-328 private port: in exported JS, `List.range 5000 == List.range 5000` throws 'Maximum call stack size exceeded'; a derived `==` on a record holding a 10,000-element list overflows the same way. List.decEq should be a loop, like the SW-336 TRMC list builders.

```
import Std (Bool, List)

type Item = { id: Nat, name: Text }

def items (n: Nat): List Item := (List.range n).map (i => { id = i, name = "x" })

export def sameNats (n: Nat): Bool := List.range n == List.range n

export def sameItems (n: Nat): Bool := items n == items n
```
